{"tool_name":"chipseeker","contents":["bioconda","bioconductor","biotools","oeb"],"identifiers":["biotools:chipseeker","bioconda:bioconductor-chipseeker","bioconductor:chipseeker"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"bioconductor-chipseeker","version":"1.46.1","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/html/ChIPseeker.html","license":"Artistic-2.0","summary":"ChIPseeker for ChIP peak Annotation, Comparison, and Visualization","identifiers":["biotools:chipseeker"],"description":"This package implements functions to retrieve the nearest genes around the peak, annotate genomic region of the peak, statstical methods for estimate the significance of overlap among ChIP peak data sets, and incorporate GEO database for user to compare the own dataset with those deposited in database. The comparison can be used to infer cooperative regulation and thus can be used to generate hypotheses. Several visualization functions are implemented to summarize the coverage of the peak experiment, average profile and heatmap of peaks binding to TSS regions, genomic annotation, distance to TSS, and overlap of peaks or genes.","build":{"noarch":"generic","number":0,"rpaths":["lib/R/lib/","lib/"],"run_exports":""},"requirements":{"host":["bioconductor-annotationdbi >=1.72.0,<1.73.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-enrichplot >=1.30.0,<1.31.0","bioconductor-genomeinfodb >=1.46.0,<1.47.0","bioconductor-genomicfeatures >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-rtracklayer >=1.70.0,<1.71.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","bioconductor-txdb.hsapiens.ucsc.hg19.knowngene >=3.22.0,<3.23.0","r-aplot","r-base","r-boot","r-dplyr","r-ggplot2","r-gplots","r-gtools","r-magrittr","r-plotrix","r-rcolorbrewer","r-rlang","r-scales","r-tibble","r-yulab.utils >=0.2.0"],"run":["bioconductor-annotationdbi >=1.72.0,<1.73.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-enrichplot >=1.30.0,<1.31.0","bioconductor-genomeinfodb >=1.46.0,<1.47.0","bioconductor-genomicfeatures >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-rtracklayer >=1.70.0,<1.71.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","bioconductor-txdb.hsapiens.ucsc.hg19.knowngene >=3.22.0,<3.23.0","r-aplot","r-base","r-boot","r-dplyr","r-ggplot2","r-gplots","r-gtools","r-magrittr","r-plotrix","r-rcolorbrewer","r-rlang","r-scales","r-tibble","r-yulab.utils >=0.2.0"]},"source_url":["https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/ChIPseeker_1.46.1.tar.gz","https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/Archive/ChIPseeker/ChIPseeker_1.46.1.tar.gz","https://bioarchive.galaxyproject.org/ChIPseeker_1.46.1.tar.gz","https://depot.galaxyproject.org/software/bioconductor-chipseeker/bioconductor-chipseeker_1.46.1_src_all.tar.gz"],"test":{"commands":["$R -e \"library('ChIPseeker')\""]},"parent_recipe":{"name":"bioconductor-chipseeker","path":"recipes/bioconductor-chipseeker","version":"1.16.1"}},"biotools":{"id":"chipseeker","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/ChIPseeker","license":"Artistic-2.0","summary":"This package implements functions to analyze multi-omics epigenetic data. Data of fragment type and base type are supported by epiSeeker. It provides functions to retrieve the nearest genes around the peak, annotate genomic region of the peak, statistical methods to estimate the significance of overlap among peak data sets, and motif analysis. It incorporates the GEO database for users to compare their own dataset with those deposited in the database. The comparison can be used to infer cooperative regulation and thus can be used to generate hypotheses. Several visualization functions are implemented to summarize the coverage of the peak experiment, average profile and heatmap of peaks binding to TSS regions, genomic annotation, distance to TSS, overlap of peaks or genes, and the single-base resolution epigenetic data by considering the strand, motif, and additional information.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T14:55:17Z","last_update_date":"2026-11-22T22:40:13.744054Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.48.1"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:chipseeker","credit":[{"name":"Chun-Hui Gao","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Guangchuang Yu","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer","Maintainer"]},{"name":"Herv\u00e9 Pag\u00e8s","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Michael Kluge","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Ming Li","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Qianwen Wang","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Thomas Schwarzl","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Yun Yan","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Zhougeng Xu","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/ChIPseeker"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence coordinates","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2012"},"format":[{"term":"BED","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3003"},{"term":"Sequence range format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2078"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"Gene-set enrichment analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2436"},{"term":"Sequence annotation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0361"},{"term":"Sequence feature comparison","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0256"},{"term":"Visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0337"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Nucleic acid features","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1276"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]},{"data":{"term":"Plot","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2884"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]}]}],"name":"ChIPseeker","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1002/cpz1.585","metadata":{"abstract":"In many aspects of life, epigenetics, or the altering of phenotype without changes in sequences, play an essential role in biological function. A vast number of epigenomic datasets are emerging as a result of the advent of next-generation sequencing. Annotation, comparison, visualization, and interpretation of epigenomic datasets remain key aspects of computational biology. ChIPseeker is a Bioconductor package for performing these analyses among variable epigenomic datasets. The fundamental functions of ChIPseeker, including data preparation, annotation, comparison, and visualization, are explained in this article. ChIPseeker is a freely available open-source package that may be found at https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/ChIPseeker. \u00a9 2022 Wiley Periodicals LLC. Basic Protocol 1: ChIPseeker and epigenomic dataset preparation Basic Protocol 2: Annotation of epigenomic datasets Basic Protocol 3: Comparison of epigenomic datasets Basic Protocol 4: Visualization of annotated results Basic Protocol 5: Functional analysis of epigenomic datasets Basic Protocol 6: Genome-wide and locus-specific distribution of epigenomic datasets Basic Protocol 7: Heatmaps and metaplots of epigenomic datasets.","authors":[{"name":"Chen M"},{"name":"Hu E"},{"name":"Li L"},{"name":"Li M"},{"name":"Wang Q"},{"name":"Wu T"},{"name":"Xie W"},{"name":"Xie Z"},{"name":"Xu S"},{"name":"Yu G"},{"name":"Zhan L"}],"citationCount":441,"date":"2022-10-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Current protocols","title":"Exploring Epigenomic Datasets by ChIPseeker."},"pmid":"36286622"},{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btv145","metadata":{"abstract":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>ChIPseeker is an R package for annotating ChIP-seq data analysis. It supports annotating ChIP peaks and provides functions to visualize ChIP peaks coverage over chromosomes and profiles of peaks binding to TSS regions. Comparison of ChIP peak profiles and annotation are also supported. Moreover, it supports evaluating significant overlap among ChIP-seq datasets. Currently, ChIPseeker contains 15 000 bed file information from GEO database. These datasets can be downloaded and compare with user's own data to explore significant overlap datasets for inferring co-regulation or transcription factor complex for further investigation.<h4>Availability and implementation</h4>ChIPseeker is released under Artistic-2.0 License. The source code and documents are freely available through Bioconductor (http://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/ChIPseeker.html).","authors":[{"name":"He QY"},{"name":"Wang LG"},{"name":"Yu G"}],"citationCount":3894,"date":"2015-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics (Oxford, England)","title":"ChIPseeker: an R/Bioconductor package for ChIP peak annotation, comparison and visualization."},"pmid":"25765347"}],"topic":[{"term":"ChIP-seq","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3169"},{"term":"Data mining","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3473"},{"term":"Data visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0092"}],"validated":1},"bioconductor":{"name":"ChIPseeker","version":"1.48.1","license":"Artistic-2.0","summary":"ChIPseeker for ChIP peak Annotation, Comparison, and Visualization","description":"This package implements functions to retrieve the nearest genes around the peak, annotate genomic region of the peak, statstical methods for estimate the significance of overlap among ChIP peak data sets, and incorporate GEO database for user to compare the own dataset with those deposited in database. The comparison can be used to infer cooperative regulation and thus can be used to generate hypotheses. Several visualization functions are implemented to summarize the coverage of the peak experiment, average profile and heatmap of peaks binding to TSS regions, genomic annotation, distance 